Number = {3},
Pages = {1048-1063},
Month = {JUL 3},
Abstract = {Overtourism has become a significant issue in many urban destinations
   across Europe, often leading to conflicts with residents and even public
   protests. Although research on overtourism has expanded rapidly, it has
   largely focused on major destinations with high tourist volumes, leaving
   questions about its occurrence in smaller city destinations. While much
   of the existing literature emphasises the negative effects of high
   tourism intensity and approaches overtourism primarily from a managerial
   or impact-focused perspective, this case study offers a more
   context-sensitive view. It examines the city of Trier, which, despite
   its relatively small population of approximately 110,000 residents,
   experiences a tourism intensity comparable to larger destinations. Based
   on an Instagram analysis, the article demonstrates that tourists are
   spatially concentrated in the city centre. Despite the high tourism
   intensity, a resident survey conducted across different districts of the
   city indicates only minimal negative impacts on quality of life. The
   study identifies several factors contributing to this high level of
   tourism acceptance and concludes that high tourism intensity does not
   automatically lead to overtourism. Consequently, further research is
   necessary to explore the conditions under which overtourism emerges in
   diverse urban contexts, to develop a more nuanced understanding of the
   phenomenon.},
